Wait, but in a standard polygonal representation of a surface, each edge appears exactly twice (once in each direction for orientable, or both in same direction for non-orientable). Here we have:
- a appears twice: once as a, once as a^{-1}
- b appears three times: once as b, twice as b^{-1}
- c appears once

Actually, in the context of fundamental group presentations of surfaces, the standard form is:
- Orientable genus g: ⟨a₁,b₁,...,a_g,b_g | [a₁,b₁]...[a_g,b_g]⟩ where [a,b] = ab a^{-1} b^{-1}
- Non-orientable genus g: ⟨a₁,...,a_g | a₁²...a_g²⟩

xyz = yxy
Multiply on left by y^{-1}: y^{-1}xyz = xy
Multiply on right by z^{-1}: y^{-1}xy = x y z^{-1}

Hmm, but this gives b appearing 3 times. Let me double-check:
- Position 1: a
- Position 2: b
- Position 3: c
- Position 4: b^{-1}
- Position 5: a^{-1}
- Position 6: b^{-1}
